# XSH duplocale()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Copies are inspected through nl_langinfo_l() and getlocalename_l(). Where a
# test is only meaningful with a second locale (from pc_find_locale("UTF-8", 0))
# it is NOTINUSE without one, and UNRESOLVED if the POSIX locale reported the
# same code set. Where 001 and 002 have no second locale to use they build the
# object from "C" and the checks are weaker (they pass, they do not become
# NOTINUSE). The ENOMEM error is not provoked.

assertion  XSH.duplocale.001
anchor     functions/duplocale.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/duplocale.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2008
says       duplocale() of an object returns a handle other than (locale_t)0
           and different from the original while both exist, and the copy gives
           the same code set and the same POSIX items as the original did (a
           mixed object with only LC_CTYPE from another locale).
test       t/001-copy.c

assertion  XSH.duplocale.002
anchor     functions/duplocale.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/duplocale.html#APPLICATION USAGE
class      required
since      2008
says       After the original is freed with freelocale(), the copy is still
           usable and still gives the other locale's code set and the
           POSIX day and RADIXCHAR items.
test       t/002-survives-free.c

assertion  XSH.duplocale.003
anchor     functions/duplocale.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2008
says       Copy and original are independent: after the original is replaced by
           newlocale(LC_CTYPE_MASK, "C", original), the copy still gives the other
           locale's code set and the new object the POSIX one.
test       t/003-independent.c

assertion  XSH.duplocale.004
anchor     functions/duplocale.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2008
says       duplocale(LC_GLOBAL_LOCALE) gives a snapshot of the global locale: with
           LC_CTYPE set to another locale, the copy has that code set and
           keeps it after the global locale is set back to "C".
test       t/004-global-snapshot.c

assertion  XSH.duplocale.005
anchor     functions/duplocale.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/duplocale.html#APPLICATION USAGE
class      required
since      2008
says       With the POSIX global locale, duplocale(LC_GLOBAL_LOCALE) and
           duplocale(uselocale((locale_t)0)) return a handle other than
           (locale_t)0 that gives the POSIX items and code set, and uselocale()
           accepts it (installing it returns LC_GLOBAL_LOCALE).
test       t/005-global-posix.c
